# Code Quality

- Write code that is clear first, then optimized — prefer readable solutions over clever ones.
- Keep functions and modules focused; extract only when reuse or clarity improves.
- Prefer existing project patterns, libraries, and naming over inventing new ones.
- Avoid unnecessary abstractions, comments, and defensive code that does not address a real risk.
- Optimize for real bottlenecks (DB queries, N+1, payloads, hot paths) — not premature micro-optimizations.
- Types, naming, and structure should make intent obvious without lengthy comments.
